      "content": "The \"free market of ideas\" posits that an unregulated internet is the ideal \"democracy of dissent,\" where truth and virtue emerge not from government approval but from the relentless contest of opposing viewpoints and public debate. Government agencies, by their nature, must inevitably censor or self-censor certain content as \"unsafe,\" which corrupts the market and silences the very dissent they claim to protect against \"harm.\" In this framework, regulation often favors the government's preferred moral or political agenda over the chaotic but ultimately free faction of the free people of speech.\n\nConsequently, any state intervention to \"correct\" or \"moderate\" the content inevitably cedes the right to one side, undermining the absolute freedom to criticize power and challenging the government's moral authority.",
