      "content": "While the \"free market of ideas\" is a compelling theoretical foundation, several counter-arguments exist that you should acknowledge to make your piece more credible. Proponents of regulation often argue from a \"harm principle,\" contending that \"safe\" speech must be distinguished from \"harmful\" speech (such as illegal content or incitement to violence) to protect society and individual well-being. Additionally, the \"slippery slope\" argument is contested; supporters of regulation claim they can enforce **lawful neutrality** and only address illegal content without censoring political debate.\n\nTherefore, while your \"free speech absolutist\" stance is logically strong in a vacuum of ideal theory, most legal and ethical systems recognize the tension between absolute liberty and the need to enforce actual harm avoidance.",
